{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Adjectives agree with nouns",
      "body": "Adjectives change to match the gender and number of the noun they describe.\n\n- Adjectives ending in *-o* have four forms: *alto, alta, altos, altas* (tall).\n- Adjectives ending in *-e* or a consonant have two forms, singular and plural: *grande, grandes* (big); *difícil, difíciles* (difficult).\n- Nationality adjectives ending in a consonant add *-a*: *español, española*.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Masculine",
          "Feminine"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Singular",
            "*el chico alto*",
            "*la chica alta*"
          ],
          [
            "Plural",
            "*los chicos altos*",
            "*las chicas altas*"
          ],
          [
            "Singular",
            "*el libro grande*",
            "*la casa grande*"
          ],
          [
            "Plural",
            "*los libros grandes*",
            "*las casas grandes*"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Adjectives usually go after the noun",
      "body": "In Spanish the adjective normally follows the noun: *una casa blanca* (a white house), *un libro interesante* (an interesting book). A few very common ones can go before, like *bueno* and *malo*, which shorten before a masculine singular noun: *un buen amigo*, *un mal día*. *Grande* before a noun becomes *gran* and means \"great\": *una gran ciudad*."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Describing people with ser",
      "body": "Use *ser* + adjective for what someone is like — appearance and personality: *Ana es alta y simpática.* To make an adjective stronger, put *muy* (very) before it: *Es muy inteligente.* For \"a bit\", use *un poco*, mostly with negative qualities: *Es un poco tímido.*\n\nTo ask what someone is like: *¿Cómo es?* — *¿Cómo es tu profesora?* (What's your teacher like?)"
    }
  ],
